WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:07.770
Shale development may be the key to the US
remaining the sole superpower for the next

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:14.770
two generations.
Energy is one of the keys to global political

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.240
power.
The US is in a great position with regard

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.450
to energy. We have shale, we have gas, we
have oil, and more importantly we have competitive

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:30.250
markets—anyone can develop them. Around
the world, our rivals are in much worse positions.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:34.510
Everybody knows about Russia’s problems,
Venezuela’s problems, but even China—thought

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:39.370
to be an economic powerhouse—is far behind
the United States. The US is the number one

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:43.540
producer of energy in the world, and China
is now the number one importer. The reason

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.440
for that is China doesn’t allow competition.
It doesn’t allow private property rights.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.380
As a result, production of coal and crude
oil are down requiring more imports even if

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:57.340
economic growth slows. So while US gas production
is soaring, Chinese gas imports are soaring.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:03.719
In 2008 at the height of the financial crisis,
it seemed as if US global was fading away.

00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:08.740
Now we’re much sought after as an ally,
cooperation in energy, trade, US energy production

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.950
being shipped overseas.
While the US will remain committed to the

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:15.450
Middle East, we don’t need to be dependent
anymore on Middle Eastern energy producers

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:21.030
like Saudi Arabia and Iran. In contrast, China
is now the largest importer from the Middle

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:24.770
East and could become enmeshed in the same
kind of problems in the Middle East that have

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.200
plagued the United States for the last forty
years.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.390
There are plenty of risks to this. Here in
the United States we could just decide not

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:35.920
to produce as much energy as we have been.
We could do that for environmental reasons

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.670
or just for political reasons. In China, they
could return to a path of market reform making

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:44.479
the Chinese economy and energy sector much
stronger.

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:48.390
Even if everything works out the way we expect
in the US and China, other developments could

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:53.140
occur such as China moving closer to Russia,
threatened by the revitalization of American

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:57.950
power.
Seven years ago, Goldman Sachs predicted $200

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:02.579
a barrel oil. That turned out to be completely
wrong. What has also turned out to be completely

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:07.640
wrong is the inevitable decline of America
and the rise of China. In fact, the exact

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.590
opposite is occurring: the US is rising and
China is stagnating.

